Let the extensions button decide for itself when the native UI comes back.

With the shell drawing the browser (browserChrome: shell), ToolbarView
hides its children and remembers which were visible, so that switching
back to Chromium's own UI can put them back. It remembers them at the
moment the shell takes over -- which at startup is before extensions have
loaded, when ExtensionsToolbarDesktop is hidden because it has nothing to
show. Switching to the native UI then restored it to hidden, and with it
the only place in the native UI to run an extension: no puzzle button, no
pinned icons, and the menu's Extensions submenu has only Manage and the
store, as upstream.

The container's visibility is not state to restore; it is a function of
whether there are any extensions, and it has UpdateContainerVisibility()
to recompute it. So after putting the snapshot back, ask it.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch
and ohos-browser-chrome-shell.patch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/views/toolbar/toolbar_view.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/views/toolbar/toolbar_view.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/toolbar/toolbar_view.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/toolbar/toolbar_view.cc
@@ -1303,4 +1303,11 @@
   ohos_saved_child_visibility_.clear();
+  // Snapshotted when the shell took over, which at startup is before any
+  // extension has loaded -- so the snapshot says hidden. Whether it shows is
+  // the container's own decision (it shows when there are extensions), made
+  // now rather than recalled from then.
+  if (extensions_container_) {
+    extensions_container_->UpdateContainerVisibility();
+  }
   if (ohos_mobile_toolbar_spacer_) {
     ohos_mobile_toolbar_spacer_->SetVisible(false);
   }
